# APZHUB Step 1 — Locked foundation (non-negotiable)

APZHUB is a **desktop-style web application**, not a generic dashboard and not a marketing site with tools attached.

## 1.1 Core application shape (persistent shell)

The default shell for the whole platform must feel like a **modern desktop workspace** with stable, familiar structure:

- **Header** — top, always visible  
- **Footer** — bottom, always visible  
- **Left side rail** — primary navigation and app switching  
- **Optional secondary rail/panel** — local navigation when a section needs it  
- **Central work canvas** — active workspace  
- **Right utility panel** — contextual information where needed  
- **Resizable split-view panels** — in the main workspace  

The shell must feel stable every day so users always know where they are.

## 1.2 Separate theme model (full token sets, not tints)

Each theme is **first-class** with its own full token set (not one theme recolored). Required tokens include: app background, elevated surfaces, panel backgrounds, borders, text hierarchy, accent colors, hover, selected, focus, and status colors.

**Light themes**

- Mist Blue  
- Sage Green  
- Soft Charcoal  

**Dark themes**

- Graphite  
- Obsidian  

Dark mode must be polished: layered dark surfaces, controlled contrast, no ugly flat black blocks unless Obsidian uses them deliberately; muted accents that still read clearly.

## 1.3 Experience separation (one shell, two compositions)

- **End-user workspace** — desktop/workbench style, productivity-oriented, panels and split views, summary and launch oriented.  
- **Admin home** — clearly distinct, operational/control-focused (tables, matrices, provisioning, audit, connector health); can still launch all services.  

Same technical shell; different composition and emphasis.

## 1.4 Engineering standards (mandatory from day one)

- **Strict TypeScript**  
- **Strict ESLint**  
- No `any` except at unavoidable external typing boundaries, explicitly contained  
- No silent type bypassing  
- No dead code  
- No vague TODO scaffolding left across the codebase  

Code must be production-shaped from the start.

## 1.5 Testing standard (starts immediately)

From the beginning the project must include:

- **Unit tests**  
- **Integration tests**  
- **Playwright** end-to-end tests  

Rules:

- Every completed feature ships with tests.  
- Every route/view/state transition must be testable from the start.  
- No fake “we’ll wire this later” screens.

## 1.6 No placeholders rule

No stubs, placeholders, fake widgets, or empty cards promising the future.

A screen or component may exist only if:

- it is **fully implemented**, or  
- it is **waiting on a real dependency** that is clearly identified  

If waiting on a real dependency: explicit disabled state, clear reason, no broken interactions, no fake content pretending to be real.

## 1.7 UI quality rule

UI must be modern, intuitive, low clutter, spacious but efficient, consistent spacing and component behavior, **desktop-like not website-like**. Header, footer, rails, panels, split views, and dialogs belong to one coherent design system.

## 1.8 Recommended implementation baseline

- Next.js  
- TypeScript  
- Tailwind CSS  
- shadcn/ui  
- Radix UI  
- TanStack Table  
- Playwright  
- Vitest or Jest  
- Strict linting and formatting in CI  

## Locked Step 1 statement (summary)

**APZHUB is defined as a desktop-style, role-assembled workspace platform with a persistent shell, five separate user-selectable themes (each a full theme, not a tint), strict TypeScript/ESLint standards, full testing from day one, and no stubs or placeholder implementation.**
